Architecture source of truth: @PLAN.md. When this rule and PLAN.md seem to
conflict, PLAN.md wins — flag the conflict instead of improvising.

Backend: Python 3.12 + FastAPI + httpx + uvicorn. These four are the entire
backend dependency core; new runtime dependencies need owner approval first.

Inference: llama.cpp `llama-server` instances managed by llama-swap. The one
inference surface is llama-swap's OpenAI-compatible endpoint, reached through
`app/llm_client.py`. The `model` field in the request selects the model;
llama-swap performs all loading and swapping, so the app contains zero
scheduler or model-lifecycle code. A second box, if it ever exists, runs its
own llama-swap; `config.yaml` maps model alias → endpoint, so it is additive.

Model placement (Phase-0 measured, @PLAN.md §4.1): big models are pinned solo
to GPU0 with `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` in the llama-swap entry's `env:`. Do not
emit `--tensor-split` — a degenerate split measured ~3x slower than a clean
single-device restriction, and a real split starves the 3070 of the VRAM its
resident models need. The `groups:` block is required, not cosmetic: without
it every model shares llama-swap's implicit default group and the residents
cannot coexist with the big model.

Thinking-capable models are suppressed with llama-server's `--reasoning off`
(or `--reasoning-budget 0`), never with a `/no_think` prompt suffix — the
suffix works on some checkpoints and silently fails on others, returning an
empty `content` with the whole budget spent in `reasoning_content`. Any
structured-output or classification call sets the flag and a real
`max_tokens` (>=1024; >=4096 for reasoning work).

Model names, aliases, and routing labels come from `config.yaml`, resolved at
runtime — this enables zero-code model swaps and keeps prompts model-agnostic.

Frontend: TypeScript compiled with plain `tsc` to native ES modules the
browser loads directly. `tsc` is the entire build; UI structure comes from
hand-written modules (`mount(el, state)` / `unmount()`), keeping every view
small and framework-free. Vendored small libs only: marked, DOMPurify,
highlight.js.

Storage: one SQLite file (WAL) + FTS5 for chats, messages, memories, chunk
text, and debug traces; **Qdrant** holds vectors. Vector access goes through
the `VectorStore` interface — that interface is why the Phase-0 result
(sqlite-vec KNN p95 105ms at 100k vectors, against a ~50ms bar) was a config
swap and not a rewrite. `SqliteVecStore` stays in-tree as the fallback
implementation and the second target of the conformance suite; no caller
outside `app/db/vectors.py` knows which store is live.

Streaming: SSE end-to-end. Every stream terminates with a `done` or `error`
event — consumers rely on this contract.
